1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,950
Why is a Costco hot dog
still a dollar fifty…

2
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:04,460
forty years later?

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:07,790
A top Costco executive once
pushed to raise the price.

4
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:09,200
They were losing money on it.

5
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:10,610
The co-founder's answer?

6
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:14,300
"If you raise the price of
the hot dog, I will kill you."

7
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:15,990
Think about how strange that is.

8
00:00:16,090 --> 00:00:18,050
Since 1985, rent is up.

9
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:19,190
Wages are up.

10
00:00:19,190 --> 00:00:20,390
Beef is up.

11
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:23,100
Gas, bread, coffee, houses.

12
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:27,060
A dollar fifty back then is worth more
than four and a half dollars today.

13
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:28,890
And the Costco hot dog?

14
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:30,340
Still a dollar fifty.

15
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:32,480
Your instinct says that's crazy.

16
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:34,860
When something loses
money, you raise the price.

17
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,250
That's how business works.

18
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:41,140
So why has one of the most successful
retailers on Earth spent forty years refusing

19
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:43,500
to charge you one cent more for a hot dog?

20
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:47,270
The answer is one of the smartest
business decisions ever made.

21
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:51,080
And it turns out the hot dog was
never really the product at all.

22
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:51,950
Here's why.

23
00:00:52,470 --> 00:00:56,000
Costco opened its first warehouse in 1983.

24
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,160
Within a couple of years, a simple hot
dog cart was selling a quarter-pound,

25
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:04,030
all-beef hot dog with a can of soda
for one dollar and fifty cents.

26
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:06,620
That price has fought
two enemies ever since.

27
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:08,470
The first enemy is inflation.

28
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:11,520
Every year, a dollar buys a little less.

29
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:16,170
Today's dollar fifty is worth about
a third of what it was in 1985.

30
00:01:16,170 --> 00:01:18,360
The second enemy is cost.

31
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:23,040
Beef, buns, labor, electricity,
the cups, the napkins, the mustard.

32
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:27,580
Every single ingredient in that combo has
gone up, some of them many times over.

33
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:30,580
And here's the part that
makes it even stranger.

34
00:01:31,030 --> 00:01:33,050
Costco didn't just hold the price.

35
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:34,620
It made the deal better.

36
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:37,110
The hot dog got about ten percent bigger.

37
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:41,050
And the twelve-ounce can of soda turned
into a twenty-ounce fountain drink with free

38
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:41,670
refills.

39
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:46,470
And eventually, someone inside Costco
said what everyone was thinking.

40
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:51,410
Craig Jelinek, Costco's longtime chief
executive, later told a story about going to

41
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:53,730
the company's co-founder, Jim Sinegal.

42
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:58,550
Jelinek said, "Jim, we can't keep
selling this hot dog for a buck fifty.

43
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:00,500
We are losing our rear ends."

44
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:04,710
He wanted to raise the price,
reportedly to a dollar seventy-five.

45
00:02:04,710 --> 00:02:07,990
And Sinegal's answer has
become a business legend.

46
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:13,040
As Jelinek told it: "If you raise the
price of the hot dog, I will kill you.

47
00:02:13,270 --> 00:02:14,250
Figure it out."

48
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,480
Think about that for a second.

49
00:02:16,630 --> 00:02:20,710
The founder of a company worth hundreds
of billions of dollars, drawing a line in

50
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:21,540
the sand…

51
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,310
over a hot dog.

52
00:02:23,970 --> 00:02:26,140
But Sinegal wasn't being sentimental.

53
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:29,420
He'd built Costco on a
simple, almost stubborn rule.

54
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:34,290
Costco caps its markup at about fourteen
percent on brand-name products and fifteen

55
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:35,990
percent on its own Kirkland brand.

56
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:39,580
Most supermarkets mark
things up far more than that.

57
00:02:39,910 --> 00:02:44,760
Sinegal once described raising prices as
the business equivalent of taking heroin.

58
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:49,730
You do it once, and then you have to
do it again, and again, and again.

59
00:02:50,110 --> 00:02:53,270
And the next thing you know, you're
not the low-price leader anymore.

60
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:57,030
Raise prices once, and
customers start checking.

61
00:02:57,490 --> 00:03:00,680
Raise them again, and they
start shopping somewhere else.

62
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:03,030
So the price wasn't going to move.

63
00:03:03,110 --> 00:03:06,430
Which meant Costco had to
change everything else.

64
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:07,920
Here's where it gets clever.

65
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:12,830
For years, Costco bought its hot dogs
from an outside supplier, the kosher brand

66
00:03:12,830 --> 00:03:14,610
Hebrew National.

67
00:03:14,610 --> 00:03:19,030
But supplier prices kept climbing, and
by the late 2000s Costco was worried the

68
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:21,630
supplier couldn't keep up
with its enormous demand.

69
00:03:22,210 --> 00:03:26,690
So Costco did something most companies
would never do for a one-dollar-fifty item.

70
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:30,540
In 2009, it dropped Hebrew National.

71
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:32,460
Then it stopped buying
hot dogs altogether…

72
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:35,060
and started making them.

73
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:39,720
It built its own hot dog plant in California,
and later a second one in Illinois.

74
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:41,080
No middleman.

75
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:42,560
No supplier markup.

76
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:46,820
The hot dog became a Kirkland
Signature product, Costco's own brand.

77
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:50,470
And the new dog was even a
little bigger than the old one.

78
00:03:51,510 --> 00:03:52,780
Then there was the soda.

79
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:58,890
In 2013, Costco switched its food courts
from Coca-Cola to Pepsi, a deal that helped

80
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:00,440
keep the combo at a dollar fifty.

81
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:06,690
And in 2025, when Costco switched back to
Coca-Cola, the price still didn't budge.

82
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:07,910
A dollar fifty.

83
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:13,850
Then in 2026, Costco tweaked the combo
again, letting members swap the soda for a

84
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:14,970
bottle of water.

85
00:04:15,290 --> 00:04:16,150
The price?

86
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:17,490
You already know.

87
00:04:17,970 --> 00:04:18,840
A dollar fifty.

88
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:24,660
Every time costs rose, Costco
attacked the cost instead.

89
00:04:25,090 --> 00:04:27,390
Anything except the number on the sign.

90
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:32,540
So why would a giant company build
entire factories to protect a hot dog?

91
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:36,920
The answer is that Costco isn't really
in the business of selling you things.

92
00:04:37,230 --> 00:04:39,520
It's in the business of
selling you a membership.

93
00:04:40,050 --> 00:04:41,190
Look at the numbers.

94
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:46,000
In fiscal 2025, Costco brought in more
than five billion dollars just from

95
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:47,170
membership fees.

96
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:51,270
That's roughly half of the
company's entire operating profit.

97
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:55,050
Before you've bought a single item, Costco
has already made a huge share of its

98
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:55,400
money.

99
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:57,010
And the hot dog?

100
00:04:57,350 --> 00:05:02,800
In 2025, Costco sold about two hundred
and forty-five million hot dog combos.

101
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:06,620
That's around three hundred and
sixty-seven million dollars.

102
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:07,820
It sounds like a lot.

103
00:05:08,070 --> 00:05:12,070
But for a company with more than two hundred
and seventy billion dollars in revenue,

104
00:05:12,070 --> 00:05:13,350
it's a rounding error.

105
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:15,580
A tiny fraction of one percent.

106
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,030
So the hot dog doesn't need to make money.

107
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:19,480
It has a different job.

108
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:21,610
The hot dog is a promise.

109
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:26,460
It's the cheapest, simplest, most visible
proof of the deal Costco makes with you:

110
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:27,930
we will not squeeze you.

111
00:05:27,930 --> 00:05:32,020
Every dollar-fifty lunch reminds
members they're getting a fair deal.

112
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:34,760
And people who feel that keep renewing.

113
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:39,090
More than ninety percent of Costco members
in the United States and Canada renew

114
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:40,080
every year.

115
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:43,690
A hot dog that loses a little
money is cheap advertising.

116
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:46,890
A membership that renews for
decades is the real treasure.

117
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:50,110
Now this brings us to one of the
strangest parts of the whole strategy.

118
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:54,440
Because the hot dog isn't the only
thing Costco protects like this.

119
00:05:54,910 --> 00:05:56,350
There's the rotisserie chicken.

120
00:05:56,700 --> 00:06:00,320
Five dollars, or to be
exact, four ninety-nine.

121
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:03,210
That price has been frozen since 2009.

122
00:06:03,210 --> 00:06:04,900
And people buy a lot of them.

123
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:09,800
In fiscal 2025, Costco sold more than a
hundred and fifty-seven million rotisserie

124
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:10,610
chickens.

125
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:12,610
And here's the astonishing part.

126
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,530
Costco's former finance chief said the
company was willing to give up thirty to

127
00:06:16,530 --> 00:06:21,810
forty million dollars a year just to
keep that chicken at four ninety-nine.

128
00:06:21,810 --> 00:06:23,830
Most companies would
simply raise the price.

129
00:06:24,170 --> 00:06:25,630
Costco did the opposite.

130
00:06:26,110 --> 00:06:30,520
In 2019, it opened its own enormous
chicken operation in Nebraska.

131
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:34,660
A project costing more than
four hundred million dollars.

132
00:06:34,660 --> 00:06:39,580
Farms, feed, and a processing plant built
to supply up to two million chickens every

133
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:40,630
single week.

134
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:42,120
Think about that.

135
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,330
Hundreds of millions of dollars…

136
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:45,820
to protect a five-dollar chicken.

137
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,150
And it's the exact same
logic as the hot dog.

138
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:51,700
The chicken usually sits
near the back of the store.

139
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:55,920
To reach it, you walk past aisle
after aisle of everything else.

140
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,170
You came in for a five-dollar chicken.

141
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:00,910
You leave with a cart full of groceries.

142
00:07:00,910 --> 00:07:02,810
The cheap item isn't the product.

143
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:04,870
It's the reason you
walked through the door.

144
00:07:05,190 --> 00:07:10,010
So let's put the whole picture together,
and see just how wrong our instinct was.

145
00:07:10,010 --> 00:07:12,520
The dollar-fifty hot dog
looks like a mistake.

146
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:13,500
It's not.

147
00:07:13,610 --> 00:07:15,490
It's a masterpiece of strategy.

148
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:20,800
Costco builds factories, rewrites supplier
deals, and swaps soda brands, all to keep

149
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,500
one number frozen in place.

150
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:26,290
Because that number earns something
far more valuable than profit.

151
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:27,960
It earns trust.

152
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:32,550
So the next time you hand over a dollar
fifty and wonder how on earth they're making

153
00:07:32,550 --> 00:07:34,710
money on this, remember the truth.

154
00:07:35,110 --> 00:07:35,880
They're not.

155
00:07:36,230 --> 00:07:37,460
And they don't need to.

156
00:07:37,930 --> 00:07:39,540
You're not buying a hot dog.

157
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:41,780
You're renewing your trust.

158
00:07:42,110 --> 00:07:46,220
Costco's hot dog proves that customers
will love you for refusing to change.

159
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:52,110
But in 1985, the very same year that
dollar-fifty hot dog was born, another American

160
00:07:52,110 --> 00:07:54,310
giant learned the opposite
lesson the hard way.

161
00:07:54,310 --> 00:07:57,300
Coca-Cola changed its formula.

162
00:07:57,750 --> 00:08:00,880
In blind taste tests, people
actually liked the new one better.

163
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:05,020
And it still became one of the
biggest disasters in business history.

164
00:08:05,490 --> 00:08:07,120
That story is right here.
